When running Linux under WSL2, it is not enough to do sudo ncu because this fails with permissions error ERR_NVGPUCTRPERM. To enable it:
* Run the NVIDIA Control Panel in Windows as administrator
* Turn on "Enable Developer Settings" in the Desktop menu
* Click on Manage GPU Performance Counters in the (newly appearing) Developer section of the left sidebar
* Select "Allow access to the GPU performance counters to all users" and click the Apply button
* Choose to keep the new setting before the automatic revert timer expires
* Now you can run ncu in Linux under WSL (and no sudo is needed)
* When finished, restore the default "Restrict access to the GPU performance counters to admin users only" setting

The Section 5 commands for Round 7 were:

$       COARSE_FILL=0 COARSE_PIPE=0 COARSE_TEST_STOP_AFTER_CHUNKS=124 ncu --set full -k regex:k_logs_batch \
          --launch-skip 123 --launch-count 1 -o d44_fill0 ./coarse_sieve 136279841 44 prof \
          --load-survivors out43.survivors.txt --no-checkpoint --no-tune
      COARSE_PIPE=0 COARSE_TEST_STOP_AFTER_CHUNKS=124 ncu --set full -k regex:k_logs_batch \
          --launch-skip 123 --launch-count 1 -o d44_fill3 ./coarse_sieve 136279841 44 prof \
          --load-survivors out43.survivors.txt --no-checkpoint --no-tune
      COARSE_METHOD=rr COARSE_PIPE=0 COARSE_TEST_STOP_AFTER_CHUNKS=322 ncu --set full -k regex:k_logs_batch \
          --launch-skip 321 --launch-count 1 -o d47_rr ./coarse_sieve 136279841 47 prof \
          --load-survivors out46.survivors.txt --no-checkpoint --no-tune

After configuring the NVIDIA Control Panel as described in the first paragraph, there were problems running the above ncu commands for the first time, see below.

The above command created .ncu-rep files, which were converted to readable text form by the below commands (which were quick and had no problems):

$ ncu --import d44_fill0.ncu-rep --page details > d44_fill0.txt
$ ncu --import d44_fill3.ncu-rep --page details > d44_fill3.txt
$ ncu --import d47_rr.ncu-rep --page details > d47_rr.txt


When running the (first set of three) ncu commands for the first time there were warnings in d44_fill0.txt and d47_rr.txt:

WRN   A buffer overflow occurred, resulting in potentially inaccurate sampling data. It is recommended to increase
          the warp sampling buffer size using `--warp-sampling-buffer-size` option to address this issue. A buffer of
          size 536870912 bytes was used.
 
and an error when trying to run d44_fill3:

==ERROR== An error was reported by the counter measurement library:
==ERROR== For more information, see /tmp/nsight-compute-7310-65ee.log
==ERROR== LaunchFailed
==ERROR== For more information, see /tmp/nsight-compute-7310-65ee.log
==PROF== Trying to shutdown target application
==ERROR== The application returned an error code (9).
==PROF== Report: /home/aspen/brent/mersenne_trinomial/src/d44_fill3.ncu-rep

The file /tmp/nsight-compute-7310-65ee.log didn't really provide any more useful information.

The above problems went away after doing the following:
1) editing the .wslconfig file in /mnt/c/Users/<my username> to increase the RAM of the WSL virtual machine from 8GB back to the default 16GB (half of 32GB total RAM),
then doing wsl --shutdown from a PowerShell window, then restarting a Linux terminal
2) using --warp-sampling-buffer-size 2500000000 when running ncu for d=44
3) using --warp-sampling-buffer-size 4000000000 when running ncu for d=47


After successfully finishing the ncu profiling as described above,
initially I didn't restore the default "admin users only" setting for GPU performance counters in the NVIDIA Control Panel.
Then whan I tried to do a full ordinary run of coarse_sieve with d=51 (not under ncu), I got hanging behavior:

The video card fans went silent and there was no further output, but "top" showed CPU utilization at 100%.
There was a .ckpt.lock file which was exactly 15 minutes older than the .ckpt file (the default checkpoint interval).
Deleting the lock file manually didn't end the hanging even after waiting more than 15 minutes. Termination was required with two Ctrl-C.
However, if I recall correctly, running with --no-checkpoint still produced the hanging behavior.
Hanging typically happened after one or two "dots".
Resuming from the checkpoint (after manually deleting the .ckpt.lock file) worked, but there would soon be another hang.

The hanging behavior went away after I restored the default "admin users only" for GPU performance counters in the NVIDIA Control Panel and updated the NVIDIA driver and rebooted Windows
(the last two were just in case, I don't know if they actually contributed to the fix).

(Note: in the previous round I got hanging behavior when running coarse_sieve with d=47 under ncu. That run used --launch-skip 20 and therefore ran for much longer than the above run with d=47.
I do believe that hanging was real, because the hanging recurred in this round as described above.)
